= Kolb's experiential learning cycle — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''Kolb's experiential learning cycle''' describes a way to learn from experience: try something, examine what happened, develop an explanation, and test an improved approach. David A. Kolb presented the model in his 1984 book ''Experiential Learning''.<ref name="kolb">D. A. Kolb, [https://books.google.com/books/about/Experiential_Learning.html?id=ufnuAAAAMAAJ ''Experiential Learning: Experience as the Source of Learning and Development''], Prentice-Hall (1984).</ref> The four parts are useful questions for designing a learning activity, not a rule that every person must follow four steps in a fixed order.

== The four modes ==
# '''Concrete experience:''' participate in an event or task and notice its circumstances.
# '''Reflective observation:''' review what happened, including surprises and alternative interpretations.
# '''Abstract conceptualization:''' form a principle, explanation, or tentative hypothesis.
# '''Active experimentation:''' apply that idea in another attempt and see what changes.

== Example: learning to calibrate a sensor ==
A student measures a sample and notices inconsistent readings (experience). They compare readings and record when the sensor drifted (reflection). They hypothesize that temperature changes affect the result (conceptualization). They repeat the procedure with temperature controlled (experimentation). The next observations may revise the hypothesis; they do not automatically prove it.

== What the model does not establish ==
The four modes are a useful planning vocabulary, but a class may involve several modes simultaneously. The related claim that each learner has a stable ''learning style'' requiring specially matched instruction has not been demonstrated by robust evidence. A teacher can provide varied activities without assigning students to types. Reviews distinguish a learner's preference from evidence that matching instruction to that preference improves learning.<ref name="styles">H. Pashler, M. McDaniel, D. Rohrer and R. Bjork, [https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2009.01038.x Learning Styles: Concepts and Evidence], ''Psychological Science in the Public Interest'' 9, 105–119 (2008).</ref>

